WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.860
In 2019, Steven Wilson, founder of the
high-performing Ascend charter school

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.500
network, wrote that efforts to make
schools more "equitable" and "inclusive"

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.050
could wind up morphing into lowered
expectations. Wilson wrote that the civil

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:24.140
rights community had sometimes erred in
allowing rigor to be seen as racist and

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:29.940
argued that intellectual joy is good for
all students of every race and background.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.930
Astonishingly, Wilson's lofty
sentiments were labeled as "white

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:38.850
supremacist rhetoric" by an online
petition, a galling accusation given

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:44.309
Wilson's lifetime of work expanding
educational opportunity. Yet, in response

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:51.360
to the petition, Ascend fired Wilson. The
charter school community responded with silence.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.560
The whole episode raises
questions about whether charter schools

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.300
will choose groupthink over bedrock
educational principles. For a sector that

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:06.200
has been defined by bold, innovative
leadership, the answer will prove telling.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.340
Do you think groupthink is becoming a
problem in American education? Let us

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.720
know in our poll. Also, let us know what
other topics you'd like our scholars to

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:19.979
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to LIKE
and subscribe for more research and

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:23.000
videos from AEI.

